From f6a4a666491cf66ed6cb37adc70b55a66b45cda1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 17:34:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(cron): scan scripts behind escaped subscript
 substitutions in arithmetic words [daveey]
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`let`, `[[ … -eq … ]]` and `declare -i` operands are shell words first: bash
removes quotes and backslashes, then evaluates the result as arithmetic, and
an array subscript there runs its command substitution. With
`let x=a[\$\(S\)]` (or `let x=a[\`S\`]`, `let x=a['$'\(S\)]`,
`[[ 1 -eq a\[\$\(S\)\] ]]`, …), bash 5.2.21 runs S. The raw text shows no
substitution, though, so the referenced-script walk never read S, and an S
holding `hermes gateway restart` was allowed (fail-open, t_01a1db0af44d1601).

Fix: new cron/lifecycle_guard_arith_words.py. For each tokenized (so
dequoted) segment it takes the operands of an integer operator, the
arguments of `let` (behind reserved words and transparent wrappers such as
`builtin`), and the arguments of an integer declaration. Every operand that
holds a `[` and a `$(`/backtick goes back to the walk as shell text, with
backtick spans spelled `$( … )`. iter_recursive_payloads yields these next
to the `sh -c` payloads. A cheap prefilter (a `[` plus a `$` or backtick)
skips the extra tokenization for most commands. A payload only makes the
walk read the file: the guard refuses only when that file holds a lifecycle
command or cannot be scanned. It is independent of
output_reaches_shell / shell_arith_sink, so it also works on main before the
quoted-subscript sink fix (t_01a1dace884ce45d) lands.

Proof:
- tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_escaped_words.py: 12 shapes where
  real bash runs a marker script. They cover the 7 `_GUARD_GAPS` shapes
  t_01a1dace884ce45d carries as strict xfail, plus `let a[…]=1`,
  `builtin let`, `if let`, `declare -i` and `[[ … && … -eq … ]]`. Each one
  is refused when the script holds `hermes gateway restart` and allowed
  for a clean twin. 5 controls bash does NOT run (echo, `let x=\$\(S\)`
  with no subscript, plain `declare`, `[[ == ]]`) stay allowed.
- RED on main (hook removed): 12 failed, 17 passed. GREEN: 36 passed.
- Mutants: 13/13 killed (each clause, the hook, the dedup and the bounds),
  plus a tokenization tripwire for the prefilter.
- All 39 test files that reference lifecycle_guard: 2668 passed. One run of
  test_large_no_sink_heredoc_scan_stays_fast went over its 5 s wall at host
  load 11. It passes alone. A direct timing of that input shows 2.07 s with
  the hook and 1.85 s without.
- Not covered: `declare -i x; x=a[\$\(S\)]`, a later assignment to an
  integer variable (that needs variable-attribute state). The guard also
  never follows backticks in general (`echo \`S\``); sibling
  t_01a1db0c38d72914 owns that.

Rebased onto main with the quoted-subscript sink fix (0e60b2c435, PR #3062):
- Dropped the 7 strict `_GUARD_GAPS` xfail marks for this card in
  tests/cron/test_lifecycle_guard_arith_word_quotes.py; they now XPASS.
  `let 'a[`SCRIPT`]=1'` (filed under t_01a1db0c38d72914) also passes now
  because backtick spans in dequoted operands are spelled `$( … )`, so its
  mark is gone too; `let "a[\`SCRIPT\`]=1"` stays strict xfail for that card.
- The `echo let x=a[\$\(S\)]` guard-level control moved to the payload-level
  table: on main the coarse printed-sink check already refuses it (fail-closed,
  independent of this change), and this module yields no payload for it.
- Rebased run: both files 93 passed + 1 xfail; all 40 test files referencing
  lifecycle_guard / shell_arith_sink: 2726 passed.
